olution  that's seen in vitro and it's also seen  uh in vivo in this case using a  different synthetic lethal  drug the the brd inhibitor jq1  so these results have been very exciting  for our group  because again i think they point to the  idea that  although there are many roads that can  lead to resistance to any one therapy  that those roads often lead to the  convergent activation of specific  downstream resistance mechanisms like  mick  or convergence upon a particular  cellular state  and by defining what those states or  convergent mechanisms are  we might be able to define therapeutic  strategies that target them  and in so doing have the capacity to  block or specifically target  many different types of resistance so  on the heels of that excitement we've  we've been looking more broadly in our  group  for so-called conserved collateral  sensitivities  and one of the ways we've been doing  that is taking oncogene-driven cell  lines  which are initially sensitive to a  targeted therapy evolving them over the  course of months to resistance until  they're fully resistant to that therapy  and then taking those resistant  derivatives along with their parental  counterparts  and treating them with a diverse panel  of drugs targeting different cellular  processes  and looking for drugs that have specific  activity  in the setting of the resistant cells  relative to the parental cells and those  are the ones that we call collateral  sensitivities  now just to give you a very high level  snapshot view of our findings  what we find is that collateral  sensitivity is a very widespread  phenomenon  so if you look across cell lines derived  from many different cancer types  aml melanoma colorectal cancer head and  neck squamous cell carcinoma  lung cancer and so on where in each case  we've evolved these cells to resistance  to the appropriate cognate targeted  therapy based on whatever the driver is  in that particular  uh cell line what you see  is that the resistant cells that emerge  are oftentimes  collaterally sensitive to other drugs  and those are the blue dots you see  below each  on below the zero point on the on the  y-axis  so this gives us a lot of hope because  it suggests that the development of  resistance to  to any one anti-cancer drug in any one  cancer type  has the potential to give rise to all  kinds of new vulnerabilities in a tumor  that can be exploited um  and and and a lot of our work is now  focusing on what these are and how we  can exploit them  so in conclusion um i hope that i've  conveyed to you the idea that  um uh that the alterations that drive  resistance to  to cancer therapies give rise to  collateral sensitivities  um that resistance mechanisms although  diverse in nature  frequently converge onto common  downstream biological mechanisms  and that as a consequence resistant  cells even those driven by  different resistance mechanisms often  share common collateral sensitivities  and then by targeting these collateral  sensitivities it may be possible to  build drug therapies that select against  resistance  um and these are just um simple  schematics depicting the idea of  convergent resistance  and collateral sensitivity with that i  want to thank you for your attention  um i want to acknowledge the folks in my  research group  who did uh the the work that i've shown  today  the antagonistic pleotropy story in aml  was  led primarily by uh kevin lynn an md phd  student in the lab  and justine rudder an undergraduate who  became subsequently a technician in the  lab and is now on her way to  phd studies the pathway activation  screening work was um  led by colin marks in the lab as well as  folks like peter winter and grace  anderson on the crispr analog version of  that our work on  convergent resistance evolution in  melanoma was driven  primarily by katie singleton a former  postdoc in the lab  and lauren crawford a former graduate  student in the lab  our our work is made possible beca